# ---------------------------------------------------------------- to own the roof over our heads (W B, cycle s4)

== the small house at the end of the road
tier: life event
aim: to own the roof over our heads
aim_role: spark
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 20-60
per_year: 0 0 .4 .4 .3 0
tone: mixed
life: home, money
horizon: months
roles: partner, parent, friend, an estate agent
earth: a small terraced house for sale at the end of the road, and a deposit almost saved
tribal: a sheltered hollow by the river where a lodge could stand, and the hides almost gathered to build it
magic: a narrow house inside the city walls offered for sale, and the deed-price almost saved
scene: There is a small house for sale at the end of the road, two up and two down, and {N} has walked past it every evening for a month. The savings are close, but not quite there.
scene W: {N} has put by the same sum every month for years, and never missed one.
scene U: {N} has the mortgage tables, the survey costs and three different plans spread across the kitchen table.
scene B: Houses on this road will be worth double in ten years, and {N} intends to be in early.
scene R: {N} has already pictured the front door painted a bold yellow.
scene G: {N} grew up moving from one rented place to the next, and wants a door that stays.
- W | keep to the saving plan, and offer only when the full deposit is there | v: security, conformity | dream: test | foot: means money+.05 | chance: 65
- U | read the survey report end to end before saying a single word | v: security | foot: lesson read the small print twice | chance: 82
- B | put in a low offer today and let the seller sweat | v: power, achievement | dream: test | foot: person rival | chance: 45
- R | borrow the last of it from friends and make an offer tonight | v: stimulation | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 55
- G | ask the family whether they can help, as families always have | v: tradition, security | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 60
- W>G | take on a second job for six months to close the gap | v: security, achievement | foot: means time-.08 | chance: 58
- U>W | sit down with a mortgage adviser and plan to own it outright in twenty years | v: security, conformity | foot: place comm.credit | chance: 75
- B>U | find out who owns the house and why they are selling, before making a move | v: power, achievement | foot: lesson know who is selling | chance: 70
- R>B | sell the car and put all of it into the deposit | v: achievement, stimulation | foot: means freedom-.05 | chance: 72
- G>R | let this one go, sure that the right house will come along | v: self-direction | dream: drop | foot: door turned down a chance | chance: 90
works: The keys come in a small paper envelope, and {N} stands on the doorstep for a long time before going in.
works: The offer is accepted on a wet Tuesday, and {N} rings everyone in the phone book.
fails: Someone else offers more, and the sold sign goes up on Saturday.
fails: The survey finds the roof is rotten, and the plan is off for another year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- to make the rules everyone lives by (W B, cycle s1)

== the yard that needs new rules
tier: life event
aim: to make the rules everyone lives by
aim_role: spark
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-85
per_year: 0 0 .3 .4 .4 .3
tone: mixed
life: community, public life
horizon: months
roles: rival, friend, neighbours, the chair of the residents' meeting
earth: the residents' meeting votes for new house rules, and someone has to draft them
tribal: the camp council wants new rules for the shared hunting grounds, and someone has to speak them first
magic: the lane's guild-moot wants new rules for the shared well, and someone has to draft the charter
scene: The residents' meeting has voted, at last, for new rules on the shared yard: bins, bikes, noise and parking. The chair looks round for someone to draft them, and {Ns} hand is already halfway up.
scene W: {N} believes a good rule that everyone keeps is worth ten arguments.
scene U: {N} has read the rules of six other blocks and seen what works.
scene B: Whoever writes the rules decides how the yard is run, and {N} means it to be run well.
scene R: {N} can already hear the complaints, and is itching for the fight.
scene G: The yard used to run on goodwill, and {N} wants rules that keep the goodwill in them.
- W | draft the rules in plain words, and read them out at the next meeting | v: conformity, universalism | dream: test | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 70
- U | knock on every door first and ask what each household needs | v: universalism | foot: person neighbour | chance: 65
- B | write the rules alone, and call for a vote that same night | v: power | dream: test | foot: person rival | chance: 52
- R | stand up and argue against any rules at all, for the fun of it | v: stimulation, self-direction | foot: mark defied an authority | chance: 60
- G | say the yard needs no rules, only a barbecue to get everyone talking | v: benevolence | dream: drop | foot: means ties+.05 | chance: 70
- W>U | borrow the best rules from other blocks, and adapt them line by line | v: conformity, achievement | foot: lesson good rules are often borrowed | chance: 75
- U>B | add a clause that whoever drafted the rules chairs the yearly review | v: power | foot: place rule.voice | chance: 55
- B>R | win over the loudest neighbours before the vote, so the rules sail through | v: power, achievement | foot: person go-between | chance: 62
- R>G | hold the meeting out in the yard itself, so everyone argues on the spot | v: stimulation, benevolence | foot: lesson arguing outdoors clears the air | chance: 68
- G>W | write in a rule that every new neighbour gets a welcome visit | v: benevolence, tradition | foot: door kept your word | chance: 80
works: The rules pass on a show of hands, and a year later even the grumblers keep them.
works: A copy of {Ns} rules turns up pinned in the next block's hallway, word for word.
fails: The vote is lost, and {N} becomes the neighbour everyone avoids on the stairs.
fails: The rules pass, nobody keeps them, and the bins stay where they always were.
